    "hypothesis": "Where the total-magnetic-intensity horizontal gradient is strongest, a magnetisation contrast marks a fault; a dot sitting 1-2 px off that crest would earn more kernel credit if it climbed onto it.",
    "mechanism": "Lineaments in aeromagnetics are the surface expression of the fault plane. R0's dots come from a smooth model over the 19-band stack at 2.8 px spacing, so each dot's sub-kernel position is a model average, not an edge measurement. Snapping replaces that average with the local maximum of the TMI horizontal gradient.",
    "confounder": "Pixel-scale dither in the delivered gradient band (0 % of horizontal neighbour differences are zero), so a 100 m crest can be a resampling artefact; 400 m flight lines at 150-200 m clearance also make a 100 m peak position not a 100 m fault position. Measured pre-test: R0's dots already sit at the 62nd percentile of this surface (background 50th), so the parent has partly used this field already and the remaining headroom is small.",
    "falsifier": "A live change of +0.010 or more would falsify the pre-test claim that the magnetic edge adds nothing at 100 m beyond the parent's own field (catalogue AUC 0.527, dots already at the 62nd percentile). Inside +/-0.010 it is no evidence and closes the magnetic-snap form for Phase 1."
  },
